# Understanding Finance
    Money:
        medium of exchange (how many bottles of water are worth one bottle of coke)
        unit of value: measure the value of goods, services, performance
        store of value: saving and borrowing allows timing of consumption
# Financial markets: institutions of trading financial assets
    financial assets are like IOUs
        assets carry right to receive cash flow
    markets refer to all institutions and people who buy and sells financial assets
# Actors on financial markets
    households:
        participate by finding solutions to:
            balancing monthly budget
            deficit or surplus
            choosing patterns if consumptions
                level of affordability, choice of long term assets (rent/buy), choice of discretionary consumption (vacation)
            meeting debt obligations
    Businesses and government entities:
        managing cash flows, saving and investing excess cash, borrowing to finance investments, issuing equity capital (stocks)
        legal vs natural persons
            Natural: they are people who are born into legal institution and system
            Legal: firms and government entities, established and disbanded by other natural or legal persons by ways prescribed by law
            business and gov entities participate in financial markets by the financial management of their affairs
            Decision making delegated to professional managers
        Financial mgmt
            manage incoming and outgoing cash flow to stakeholders
            investment in short and long term assets
            financing these investments by debt or equity
            Above activities involve obligations to pay or receive present or future payments and financial contracts
    The US Treasury (as well as treasuries of other governments in the world)
        borrow money to finance federal spending when these exceed tax revenues – repay borrowings when tax revenues exceed federal spending
        a particular legal person: creates laws and institutions (legislative branch), is responsible for executing these laws (executive branch), and interpret and evaluate laws (judicial branch)
        Treasury manages
            federal finances by collecting taxes and paying bills
            currency
            gov accounts and national debt
        it is largest player on financial markets
        also enforces finance and tax laws
        The Fed:
            central bank of the US and has 12 regional branches
            overseen by board of governors in DC
            Primary duties: ensure health and stability of US economy, prevent inflation, preserve health of financial system
            it is responsible for monetary policy:
                setting fed funds rate, engaging in gov bond buying and selling, setting banks' reserve requirements
    Financial intermediaries: banks, institutional investors
        directing the flow of funds throughout the economy, managing payment systems, accepting deposits, making loans, pooling and managing financial risks
        refers to the group activities that direct money flow from savers to borrowers
        2 types: banks and non-banks
        middlemen among businesses, government entities, the Fed, and households
        Functions:
            payment transfers
            loans – not only grant loans, but also borrow themselves
            investment decisions – intermediation and trading on their own account
            risk appraisal and management
# Time Value of Money
    TVM:
        related to consumption timing: investing financial assets allows storing wealth and postponing consumption as well as borrowing and bringing consumption forward
        Difference in value of money today and in the future is called TVM
            by investing our savings in a risk free asset today, we can convert our present money to future money (interest rates allows us ot convert money from one point to another)
    The Rate
    effective appreciation of money over time, assuming that the investor does not withdraw any money
    Reward for delaying consumption
        the further in the future, the higher the FV of the money
    Effective Annual Rate (EAR) vs Annual Percentage rates
        APRs are rates quoted by banks along with frequency of compounding
        APR can be converted to any periodic rate by dividing by the frequency of compounding
            Quart: APR/4, Monthly APR/12, Semi-annual APR/2
            the power of the rate is how many times it is compounded so multiply by 12 for months and others accordingly
        EAR is the interest rate that would come out if it was compounded once a year
            EX: .5% monthly interest --> EAR = (1+.005)^12 - 1 = 6.17%
            EX2: 20% interest over 3 years --> EAR = (1+.2)^(1/3) - 1 = 6.265%
    Nominal vs Real Rate
        Difference is the rate of inflation --> general increase in prices
        Real rate of return = nominal rate of return (EAR) - inflation
        capture the appreciation in the value of goods and services that can be consumed
    Risk
        Risk and return
            risk refers to possibility that realized cash flows will be different from cash flows expected at the same time of the decision making
            main risk is default (non-payment)
        Investor Rationality
            investor is risk averse and expects compensation for facing risk
            Expected return on a risky asset > return on the risk-free asset
            compensation is the risk premium
            Risk premium = [expected return on an asset bearing a particular set of risks] – [risk-free return]
            The higher the risk, the greater the risk premium demanded by rational investors.
            In Finance we assume that financial markets are populated by rational investors.
        Return on risk-free asset
            A risk-free asset carries no uncertainty about future payments and the expected return will be realized under all conditions (E(R) = r risk free)
